/*! @brief base32 encoding and decoding module
 
`base32` module is a module for base32 encoding and decoding. Base32 is an algorithm used to encode binary data into ASCII strings, used to transmit binary data in network protocols such as mail and DNS.

This module provides two methods: `encode` and `decode`. The `encode` method is used to encode binary data into a Base32 string, and the `decode` method is used to decode a Base32 string into binary data. Below is a usage example:

 ```JavaScript
    const base32 = require('base32');
    const data = new Uint8Array([0x4e, 0x4f, 0x44, 0x45]); // 'NODE'
    const encoded = base32.encode(data); // 'KRUGKIDROV======'
    const decoded = base32.decode(encoded); // [0x4e, 0x4f, 0x44, 0x45]
    console.log(encoded, decoded); // KRUGKIDROV====== [78, 79, 68, 69]
 ```
 As you can see, the `encode` method encodes the binary data as `KRUGKIDROV======`, and the `decode` method decodes it as `[0x4e, 0x4f, 0x44, 0x45]`.
 
 Note that the length of the Base32 encoded result string is about 8/5 times the length of the original binary data, so it is not suitable for encoding large amounts of data. If you need to encode large amounts of data, it is recommended to use Base64 encoding.
  */
module base32
{
    /*! @brief Encode data in base32 way
	 @param data The data to encode
	 @return Return the encoded string
	 */
    static String encode(Buffer data);

    /*! @brief Decode string to binary data in base32 way
	 @param data The string to decode
	 @return Return the decoded binary data
	 */
    static Buffer decode(String data);
};